(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["pharmacology-i-exam-1-pharm-exam-1-master-exam-form-g"]={"title":"Pharmacology I Exam 1 Master Exam — Form G","category":"Pharmacology I","exam":"Exam 1","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"Which antifungal class acts on the cell wall rather than the membrane?","choices":["Polyenes","Azoles","Allylamines","Echinocandins"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, by inhibiting glucan synthesis in the wall. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 94"},{"q":"Ampicillin/sulbactam is the drug of choice for which problems?","choices":["Syphilis, gas gangrene and meningococcus","Neutropenic fever and hospital-acquired pneumonia","Endocarditis and osteomyelitis in penicillin allergy","Skin and soft tissue, diabetic foot, and bites"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The added Bacteroides cover is what suits bite wounds. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"Why do newer bladder antimuscarinics cause fewer central effects?","choices":["They are given only as a transdermal patch","They are designed not to cross the blood-brain barrier","They block nicotinic rather than muscarinic receptors","They are cleared much faster by the kidney"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, unlike oxybutynin and tolterodine. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 56"},{"q":"How does scopolamine differ from atropine?","choices":["Greater peripheral action at therapeutic doses","Greater central action at therapeutic doses","It has no effect on secretions","It stimulates rather than blocks muscarinic receptors"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, producing sedation and blocking short-term memory. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 53"},{"q":"How does clonidine lower blood pressure?","choices":["It blocks peripheral alpha-1 receptors","It acts centrally to reduce sympathetic outflow","It blocks beta-1 receptors in the heart","It dilates arteries directly as a nitrate"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, as an alpha-2 agonist inhibiting vasomotor centres. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 97"},{"q":"Which efferent neuron originates in a ganglion?","choices":["The preganglionic neuron","The afferent neuron","The postganglionic neuron","The somatic motor neuron"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and it is generally unmyelinated. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"Why does succinylcholine cause sustained depolarisation?","choices":["Acetylcholinesterase does not destroy it","It binds the receptor irreversibly","It blocks the sodium channel directly","It prevents acetylcholine release"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, so it stays in the cleft stimulating the receptor. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 67"},{"q":"Why are tetracyclines the usual systemic antibiotic for acne?","choices":["They are the only agents that reach the follicle","They can be used in children and pregnancy","They are safe, effective and inexpensive","They act faster than isotretinoin"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and they are the most commonly used group. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"Who receives ganciclovir?","choices":["Otherwise healthy adults with cold sores","Children with chickenpox","Transplant and immunocompromised patients","Patients with influenza A or B"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, for cytomegalovirus infection. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 125"},{"q":"What is monitored during linezolid therapy?","choices":["Creatine phosphokinase","Serum troughs before the fourth dose","Platelets","Visual fields and colour vision"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, because thrombocytopenia is the characteristic toxicity. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 72"},{"q":"Why does pooled sebum matter in acne?","choices":["It creates anaerobic conditions for bacteria","It physically stretches the follicle open","It denatures the keratin lining the duct","It attracts neutrophils directly"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, allowing Propionibacterium acnes to proliferate. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"Which metabolic disturbances follow propranolol?","choices":["Fasting hypoglycaemia and raised lipids","Hyperglycaemia and low triglycerides","Hyperkalaemia and metabolic acidosis","Hyponatraemia from fluid retention"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, with both cholesterol and triglycerides rising. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 117"},{"q":"Why can daptomycin not be used for pneumonia?","choices":["It fails to cover Streptococcus pneumoniae","It cannot be given intravenously","It is inactivated in the lung","It causes bronchospasm on administration"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, which is why it is excluded despite Gram-positive cover. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 72"},{"q":"Which haematological effects are listed for clindamycin?","choices":["Megaloblastic anaemia and agranulocytosis","Neutropenia and thrombocytopenia","Haemolysis in glucose-6-phosphate deficiency","Bone marrow suppression as the limiting toxicity"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, alongside rash and Clostridium difficile colitis. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 81"},{"q":"Which nondepolarising agent releases histamine?","choices":["Pancuronium","Vecuronium","Atracurium","Rocuronium"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, which can cause a fall in blood pressure and flushing. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 65"},{"q":"Which blood disorders follow sulfamethoxazole/trimethoprim?","choices":["Thrombocytopenia, agranulocytosis, megaloblastic anaemia","Polycythaemia and reactive thrombocytosis","Haemophilia and other inherited clotting factor loss","Iron deficiency from impaired absorption"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, grouped together as blood dyscrasias. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 85"},{"q":"Which macrolide salt is most associated with cholestatic hepatitis?","choices":["Lactobionate","Ethylsuccinate","Stearate","Estolate"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, and the reaction is rare overall. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 57"},{"q":"Which opportunistic pneumonia does sulfamethoxazole/trimethoprim treat and prevent?","choices":["Legionella pneumophila pneumonia","Mycoplasma pneumoniae infection","Aspergillus pneumonia in neutropenia","Pneumocystis jirovecii pneumonia"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, in both treatment and prophylaxis. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 84"},{"q":"Why is epinephrine added to local anaesthetic solutions?","choices":["It causes vasoconstriction and prolongs the block","It speeds the onset of anaesthesia","It reduces the pain of injection","It prevents allergic reactions to the anaesthetic"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, at about 1 part in 100,000. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 85"},{"q":"What leaks out of the fungal cell through an amphotericin B channel?","choices":["Sodium and chloride","Potassium and magnesium","Calcium and phosphate","Glucose and amino acids"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, which explains the electrolyte disturbance seen clinically. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 96"},{"q":"Why does neostigmine lack central effects?","choices":["It is rapidly destroyed in plasma","It does not enter the central nervous system","It binds only nicotinic receptors","It is given only by the topical route"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and it is also poorly absorbed from the gut. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"For a time-dependent antibiotic, what fraction of the dosing interval should exceed the minimum inhibitory concentration?","choices":["10 to 20 percent","80 to 100 percent","40 to 70 percent","Exactly 50 percent in every case"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and these agents show no post-antibiotic effect. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"What is the critical target in acne treatment?","choices":["The inflammatory nodule","The microcomedone","The sebaceous gland itself","The bacterial colony alone"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, because eliminating follicular occlusion arrests the cascade. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"Which renal problem follows acyclovir?","choices":["It crystallises in the renal tubule","It causes interstitial nephritis from allergy","It damages tubules by oxidative injury","It reduces renal blood flow directly"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, which is why hydration is maintained. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 123"},{"q":"What is suggested when the vancomycin minimum inhibitory concentration is 2 or above?","choices":["Doubling the interval between doses","Alternative therapy such as linezolid","Switching from intravenous to oral dosing","Adding an aminoglycoside for synergy"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, because the target ratio becomes hard to achieve. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 48"},{"q":"Why is propranolol risky in a patient prone to hypoglycaemia?","choices":["It causes hyperglycaemia that masks the problem","It increases insulin secretion directly","It blunts the normal physiological response to it","It prevents glucose absorption from the gut"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and it reduces glycogenolysis and glucagon secretion. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 115"},{"q":"Why does cocaine exaggerate the effects of epinephrine?","choices":["It increases adrenergic receptor numbers","It blocks monoamine oxidase in the gut","It sensitises the heart as an anaesthetic gas would","It prevents reuptake of the catecholamine"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, so the cardiovascular actions are amplified. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 88"},{"q":"What is the final step of cholinergic neurotransmission?","choices":["Degradation by acetylcholinesterase","Recycling of choline","Binding to the receptor","Storage in vesicles"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, after acetylcholinesterase degrades the transmitter. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"What are topical acyclovir and penciclovir used for?","choices":["External and perianal warts","Actinic keratoses on sun-exposed skin","Shingles across a dermatome","Recurrent orolabial herpes simplex"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, as synthetic guanine analogues. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 56"},{"q":"Which agent is most potent at beta receptors?","choices":["Epinephrine","Isoproterenol","Norepinephrine","Phenylephrine"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, ahead of epinephrine and then norepinephrine. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 77"},{"q":"When are the echinocandins used?","choices":["First-line therapy for all systemic fungal disease","Superficial dermatophyte infection of the nails","Cryptococcal meningitis requiring brain entry","Oesophageal candidiasis and refractory aspergillosis"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, including febrile neutropenia not responding to antibiotics. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 109"},{"q":"What does the afferent division of the peripheral nervous system do?","choices":["Carries signals away from the central nervous system","Controls voluntary movement of skeletal muscle","Carries signals towards the central nervous system","Operates independently of the central nervous system"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and it drives reflex regulation such as carotid sinus sensing. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"Which enzyme is the better understood fluoroquinolone target?","choices":["Topoisomerase IV, whose mechanism is clear","Dihydrofolate reductase in the folate pathway","RNA polymerase in transcription","DNA gyrase, also called topoisomerase II"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, forming a complex that induces DNA cleavage. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 74"},{"q":"Why must the body make new receptors after phenoxybenzamine?","choices":["The drug destroys the receptor protein directly","The receptors are internalised permanently","The block is irreversible and non-competitive","The drug depletes norepinephrine stores"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and that takes at least 24 hours. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 108"},{"q":"Which penicillin form is given as a single long-acting intramuscular dose?","choices":["Penicillin V potassium, marketed as Pen VK","Penicillin G given as benzylpenicillin","Penicillin G benzathine, marketed as Bicillin","Amoxicillin, marketed as Amoxil"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The benzathine salt is what makes the one-time dose possible. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"Which tetracycline is available intravenously?","choices":["Doxycycline","Tetracycline","Minocycline","All three are available intravenously"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, at 100 milligrams every 12 hours. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 62"},{"q":"Which eye changes does acetylcholine produce?","choices":["Mydriasis with cycloplegia and loss of near focus","Reduced tear production","Raised intraocular pressure","Ciliary contraction for near vision, and miosis"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, through the pupillae sphincter muscle. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"Red man syndrome belongs to which category of vancomycin problem?","choices":["An infusion-related reaction","An immunoglobulin E mediated allergy","A dose-dependent renal toxicity","A delayed hypersensitivity rash"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, grouped with fever, chills and phlebitis. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"Which organ function governs tigecycline dosing?","choices":["Hepatic function","Renal function","Cardiac output and perfusion","Thyroid function"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, unlike most agents here which are renally adjusted. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 66"},{"q":"How is extended-infusion piperacillin/tazobactam given?","choices":["3.375 to 4.5 grams every 6 hours over 30 minutes","3.375 to 4.5 grams every 8 hours over 4 hours","1 to 2 grams every 8 hours over 30 minutes","4.5 grams once daily over 60 minutes"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The standard schedule is every 6 hours over 30 minutes. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"How do macrolide metabolites cause drug interactions?","choices":["They induce CYP3A and speed metabolism","They displace drugs from plasma proteins","They block renal tubular secretion","They form an inactive complex with CYP3A"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, so substrate drug levels rise. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 59"},{"q":"Why is tyramine dangerous with a monoamine oxidase inhibitor?","choices":["It becomes a direct receptor agonist","It is normally oxidised by that enzyme in the gut","It blocks the inhibitor from working","It prevents norepinephrine being synthesised"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, so a serious vasopressor effect can follow. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 101"},{"q":"How soon does corticosteroid-induced acne appear?","choices":["Within the first 48 hours","Two to six weeks after starting therapy","After six months of continuous use","Only after the drug is stopped"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, as pustular inflammation on the trunk. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"How do tetracyclines help acne beyond killing bacteria?","choices":["They reduce sebum production directly","They correct abnormal keratinisation","They chelate calcium and block chemotaxis","They inhibit testosterone conversion"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, preventing neutrophil and monocyte movement into the lesion. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"Which agents have intrinsic sympathomimetic activity?","choices":["Acebutolol and pindolol","Atenolol and metoprolol","Timolol and nadolol","Labetalol and carvedilol"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, weakly stimulating while also antagonising. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 121"},{"q":"How does the season affect acne?","choices":["Worse in winter, better in summer","Worse in summer, better in winter","Unchanged throughout the year","Worse in spring during pollen season"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, though heat and humidity themselves induce comedones. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"Which conditions does cytomegalovirus cause?","choices":["Retinitis, oesophagitis and colitis","Chickenpox and shingles","Cold sores around the mouth","Influenza-like illness each winter"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, particularly in immunocompromised patients. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 119"},{"q":"Which topical retinoid adverse effects improve over time?","choices":["Erythema, desquamation and burning","Photosensitivity and severe sunburn","Hypopigmentation of treated skin","Bleaching of hair and clothing"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, helped further by emollients. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"How is oseltamivir dosed in a child weighing 30 kilograms?","choices":["60 milligrams twice daily","30 milligrams twice daily","45 milligrams twice daily","75 milligrams twice daily"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, for the band above 23 up to 40 kilograms. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 127"},{"q":"Where are M1 receptors found?","choices":["Cardiac cells and smooth muscle","Bladder and exocrine glands","Neurons and gastric parietal cells","Autonomic ganglia exclusively"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, while M2 sits on cardiac cells and smooth muscle. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"Which alpha blocker treats hypertensive crisis after abrupt clonidine withdrawal?","choices":["Phenoxybenzamine","Prazosin","Tamsulosin","Phentolamine"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, and it also reverses norepinephrine extravasation. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 110"},{"q":"How long does azelaic acid take to show benefit?","choices":["Two to three days","One to three months, like isotretinoin","Six months of daily use","Six to eight weeks of continuous therapy"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, and hypopigmentation is a recognised effect. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"When does influenza season generally run?","choices":["June to September","Year round without variation","December to February only","October to March"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, which frames when the drug is used. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 126"},{"q":"Which vehicle makes topical steroid absorption greatest?","choices":["Cream","Ointment","Lotion","Solution"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, with the order running ointment, then cream, then lotion. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"How is amphotericin B renal tubule damage limited?","choices":["Alkalinising the urine with bicarbonate","Giving the drug as a rapid bolus","Adding a loop diuretic to force output","Hydration with normal saline"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, alongside monitoring for uraemia and low filtration. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 99"},{"q":"Against which poisoning is pralidoxime ineffective?","choices":["Reversible inhibitors like physostigmine","Organophosphate insecticides","Agricultural nerve agents","Any cholinesterase inhibitor at all"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, since it cannot overcome that kind of inhibition. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"Which beta blockers are selective for beta-1 receptors?","choices":["Propranolol, timolol and nadolol","Labetalol and carvedilol","Atenolol, metoprolol and bisoprolol","Pindolol and phenoxybenzamine"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, useful when pulmonary function is impaired. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 120"},{"q":"How long is a medium to high potency steroid used in an exacerbation?","choices":["Until the rash has been clear a month","Continuously as maintenance therapy","A single application only","One to two weeks, then step down"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, switching to a lower potency afterwards. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"How do the benzimidazoles kill helminths?","choices":["They release acetylcholine and inhibit cholinesterase","They inhibit ergosterol synthesis","They block microtubule formation and glucose uptake","They break parasite DNA strands directly"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, which starves the parasite. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 115"},{"q":"What is the goal vancomycin trough?","choices":["1 to 2 micrograms per millilitre","10 to 15 or 15 to 20 micrograms per millilitre","30 to 40 micrograms per millilitre","5 to 10 or 20 to 25 micrograms per millilitre"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and which of the two depends on the indication. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 48"}]};
